  "summary": "I wrote the tower-stacking game in `src/main.ts`. `typecheck` passes. I could not run `vite build` or open it in a browser, so none of it has been run.\n\n- **Scene:** Aura3D scene with a perspective camera, lights and a floor. It has 26 pre-built slabs (the base plus 25) and one \"debris\" box per level. Each level has its own colour, so colours shift gradually from level to level.\n- **Sliding slab:** it moves along X and bounces between ±4.5. It starts at 2.4 units/s and gets faster every 3 levels, capped at 8.\n- **Drop:** Space or a click/tap on the canvas drops the slab. The overlap with the slab below stays, and the overhang becomes a debris piece that falls and spins away.\n- **Perfect drop:** within 0.12 of the slab below, it snaps into place at full width. It scores 2 for that slab instead of 1.\n- **Lose and win:** a complete miss makes the slab fall and sets `lost`. Reaching 25 stacked slabs sets `won`.\n- **Height and score:** the base slab is not counted. A fresh game starts at score 0 and height 0, and `height` is the number of slabs stacked on top of the base.\n- **Camera:** it is fixed. The scene scrolls down inside a \"world\" group so the top of the tower stays in view.\n- **Restart:** R at any time, or a click on the canvas after the game ends, resets to a fresh base slab.\n- **HUD and state:** a DOM HUD below the canvas shows score, best this session and status, and `window.__TASK_STATE__` is kept in sync. `window.__TASK_READY__` is set two animation frames after `app.ready()` resolves.\n\n",
